  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933730/assets/6e1d2c8fef007596dc6fd9b6ef0813fafeb7014490ff35d993f276a2a96ccb13.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            If the graph above is generated by the function $y=sin\u2061(Bx),$ then $B=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .",
  "worked_solution": "To find the value of $B,$ we first need to find the period of the function.\n\nThe graph shows that the function makes $3$ complete cycles on the interval $[0,2\\pi ].$ So, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933730/assets/544997dc11f7c88d5aa7b3f78d8dd1059a0ac08092726983897c5992d03acf8b.png)\n\n \n\nWe know that for the function $y=sin\u2061(Bx),$ the horizontal scaling factor is $\\frac{1}{B},$ and the period is multiplied by the scaling factor:\n\n$$\n\\text{period}=2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B}\n$$\n\nFor our function, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\pi }{3} & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B} \\\\ \\frac{2\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{B} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{1}{B} \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
